tantly: look at **I8 and I9** — vertical: I8(O), I9(O). Above them: I7, I6… I7 is empty, I10 is empty. So only two vertically.\n\nNow, diagonal: what about **H7(O), G8(O), F9?**  \n- H7 is O, G8 is O — that’s a diagonal going down-left: from H7 to G8 is row+1, col-1.\nNext would be F9 (col F, row 9). Is F9 occupied? No — currently empty.\nThen E10, etc.\n\nSo current diagonal: H7(O), G8(O) — two in a row on the diagonal (anti-diagonal: top-right to bottom-left).\nExtending to F9 would complete three.\n\nBut even better: what about **J10**? That would continue the same diagonal: H7(7), G8(8), F9(9), E10(10), D11(11) — but we only have two so far.\n\nAlternatively, is there a **four-in-a-row or open three** being formed by White?\n\nWait — focus on **H8, I8, I9** — not aligned.\n\nBut here’s a key possibility:\n\nLook at **J8** — if White plays J8, then G8-H8-I8-J8 becomes **four in a row horizontally**, which is a **lethal threat**: Black would have to block at F8 or K8 next turn, but if we can force a win now, better.\n\nBut we don’t have a five yet.\n\nWait — is there any **immediate five-in-a-row possible**?\n\nNo — max we have is three in a row (G8-H8-I8).\n\nBut perhaps a **fork** or **double threat**?\n\nWait — another angle: **diagonal from D4 to H8** — D4(O), but E5(X), so broken.\n\nWhat about **vertical on H column**?\nH7(O), H8(O) — two in a row. H6? Empty. H9? Empty. So just two.\n\nBut wait — is there a **winning move**?\n\nLet’s check if playing **J8** creates a four — yes: G8-H8-I8-J8 = four white stones in a row horizontally on row 8.\n\nThat would be a **direct threat to win at K8** next move — unless blocked.\n\nBut is there a way to create **two threats at once**?\n\nAlternatively, check **F9** — as part of the diagonal H7-G8-F9.\n\nH7(O), G8(O), play F9: then we have three in a diagonal (from H7 down-left).\n\nBut still not immediate win.\n\nWait — perhaps **no immediate win**, but we must look for best attacking move.\n\nBut the instructions say: “If you have any locations to immediately form a 5-in-a-row, you should play that position to win.”\n\nCurrently, **no move makes five**, because the longest white sequence is three.\n\nBut let’s double-check all directions.\n\nHorizontal on row 8: G8, H8, I8 — three. Can we extend to F8 or J8?\n\n- If we play J8: G8-H8-I8-J8 = four.\n- If we play F8: F8-G8-H8-I8 = four.\n\nEither gives four, but not five.\n\nSimilarly, vertical on I: I8, I9 — two.\n\nDiagonals:\n- Consider \\ diagonal (top-left to bottom-right): e.g., D4, E5, F6, G7, H8 — D4(O), E5(X), so broken.\n- Other \\ diagonal: F6(X), G7(X), H8(X), I9(O)? I9 is O — not same color.\n\nNow / diagonal (top-right to bottom-left): e.g., H7(O), G8(O), F9(?), E10(?), D11(?).\n\nH7(O), G8(O) — two. F9 empty.\n\nAnother / diagonal: I8(O), H9(?), G10(?), etc. — H9 empty.\n\nSo best option is to **extend the horizontal three on row 8 to four**, creating a strong threat.\n\nBut which end? F8 or J8?\n\nCheck if either extension creates a **double threat**.\n\nTry **J8**:\n- Creates G8-H8-I8-J8 (four in a row)\n- Also, J8 and I9 — not aligned\n- J8 and H7 — not aligned\n\nTry **F8**:\n- F8-G8-H8-I8 = four\n- F8 and F6? F6 is X — enemy\n- F8 and E7? E7 empty\n- F8 and G7? G7 is X — no\n\nNo obvious second threat.\n\nBut wait — what about **H9**?\n\nH9 is empty. If we play H9:\n- Vertical: H7, H8, H9 — three in a column\n- Horizontal: H9 with G9, I9 — G9 empty, I9 is O — so H9 and I9 adjacent, but not enough\n\nSo H9 would give:\n- Vertical: H7-H8-H9 (three)\n- Diagonal? H9, G8, F7 — G8 is O, F7 empty — so two on diagonal\n\nStill not better than extending to four.\n\nNow, is there a **black threat** we must block?\n\nBlack has:\n- C5, D5, E5 — three in a row horizontally on row 5 — very dangerous! This is an open three, possibly leading to four.\n- Also, E5, F6, G7, H8 — diagonal from E5 to H8: E5(X), F6(X), G7(X), H8(X) — that’s **four black stones** in a diagonal (\\ direction): E5(5), F6(6), G7(7), H8(8) — so positions: E5, F6, G7, H8 — all X!\n\nThat’s **four in a row diagonally** — a **lethal threat**!\n\nNext move, Black can play **D4**? No — D4 is already occupied by O.\n\nThe diagonal is E5, F6, G7, H8 — all X.\n\nTo complete five, they need **I9**? Let\'s see: after H8(8), next is I9(9)? Row increases, col increases — yes: I9 is row 9, col I.\n\nIs I9 occupied? Yes — by O (white)! I9 is already taken by white.\n\nSo the diagonal E5-F6-G7-H8-I9 — I9 is **already blocked by white**.\n\nSo Black cannot extend to I9 — it’s occupied by opponent.\n\nWhat about the other end? Before E5: D4 is O, C3? C3 is empty, but D4 is O — so the diagonal starts at E5, cannot extend backward.\n\nSo Black’s four-in-a-row diagonal is **blocked at both ends**: D4 is O (before E5), I9 is O (after H8). So **not a live four**, and cannot become five.\n\nSo Black cannot win next move.\n\nBut their horizontal on row 5: C5, D5, E5 — three in a row.\n\nCan they extend to B4 or F5?\n\nB4: empty. F5: empty.\n\nSo potential, but not immediate lethal.\n\nBack to White: our strongest move is to build toward five.\n\nBest structure: G8, H8, I8 — three in a row.\n\nExtending to J8 or F8 both give four.\n\nBut let’s check **J8**:\n- After playing J8, we have G8-H8-I8-J8 — four in a row\n- Threatens to play K8 next for five\n- Is K8 available? Yes\n- Would Black be able to block? Only if they respond at K8 or F8\n\nBut if we play J8, the threat is at K8, so Black must play K8 to block.\n\nBut can we instead play a move that **creates two threats at once**?\n\nConsider **H9**:\n- Completes vertical: H7-H8-H9 — three\n- And connects to I9? Horizontally: H9 and I9 — adjacent, but I9 is O, so H9-I9 is two, not three unless G9 or J9\n\nNot strong.\n\nWhat about **F9**?\n- Part of diagonal: H7-G8-F9 — if we play F9, then G8 and H7 are O, so H7-G8-F9 is a diagonal three (direction: /, i.e., top-right to bottom-left)\n- But F9 doesn\'t connect elsewhere\n\nNot better.\n\nWait — what about **J7**? Too far.\n\nAlternatively, **J8** seems strongest.\n\nBut here’s a better idea: **play J8 to create four-in-a-row**, which is a **direct winning threat** — next move we can play K8 to win, unless blocked.\n\nSince no immediate win, this is the strongest aggressive move.\n\nAlso, it pressures the center.\n\nAlternatively, **play F8** — same effect, four in a row.\n\nWhich side is better?\n\nLook at surroundings:\n- Left of G8: F8 — is F8 near black stones? F6 is X, G7 is X — so F8 might be more contested.\n- Right of I8: J8 — less crowded.\n\nAlso, Black may want to play J8 themselves? Unlikely — no black stones nearby.\n\nMoreover, if we play J8, the continuation to K8 is clear.\n\nIf we play F8, Black could potentially interfere at E5-F6 area.\n\nBut both are symmetric in value.\n\nHowever, note that **I9 is already white** — so if we could connect I8-I9 to something.\n\nBut vertical I8-I9 — only two.\n\nIf we play I10, that extends vertical — but weaker than building four.\n\nThus, **best move is to extend the three to four**.\n\nChoose **J8** — it’s open, not occupied, and creates a strong horizontal threat.\n\nConfirm: is J8 in occupied list? Occupied: D4, C5, D5, E5, F6, G7, H7, G8, H8, I8, I9 — no J8.\n\nSo valid.\n\nEvaluation:  \nWhite’s strongest structure is the horizontal three at G8-H8-I8. Playing J8 extends it to four-in-a-row, creating a direct threat to win at K8 on the next move. This is the most forcing move available. F8 would achieve a similar result but J8 is safer and less entangled with Black’s central cluster. No immediate five-move exists, and Black’s threats are blocked (e.g., diagonal E5-H8 is blocked at I9). Thus, J8 is optimal.\n\nReason: Extending the horizontal trio to a four-in-a-row maximizes pressure and creates a lethal follow-up, limiting Black’s responses.\n\nAnswer: {"col": "J", "row": 8}'}
